1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,227
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Intro to Parallel Computing. Threads,

2
00:00:04,247 --> 00:00:09,632
processes and scaling across nodes: how work gets split up, and when that actually

3
00:00:09,652 --> 00:00:10,790
makes it faster.

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:15,507
Namaste! This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. Twice the processors is not

5
00:00:15,527 --> 00:00:20,484
twice the speed. The serial part of your code, and the cost of processors talking to

6
00:00:20,504 --> 00:00:25,151
each other, set the limit before the hardware does. This lecture walks from one core

7
00:00:25,171 --> 00:00:29,507
to many GPUs across nodes: why we parallelise, the four kinds of parallel machine,

8
00:00:29,527 --> 00:00:32,929
shared against distributed memory, Amdahl's and Gustafson's laws, a real benchmark

9
00:00:32,949 --> 00:00:37,596
of Conway's Game of Life, how GPUs and PyTorch scale, and the parallel tools in

10
00:00:37,616 --> 00:00:38,840
Python, R and MATLAB.

11
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:43,675
Six terms. A core: one processing unit that runs instructions on its own. A node:

12
00:00:43,695 --> 00:00:48,779
one computer in the cluster, with its own memory. A thread: a stream of work inside

13
00:00:48,799 --> 00:00:52,927
one process, sharing that process's memory. A process: a running program with its

14
00:00:52,947 --> 00:00:56,755
own memory. MPI: the message passing interface, the standard way processes on

15
00:00:56,775 --> 00:01:00,902
different nodes send data to each other. And speedup: serial time divided by

16
00:01:00,922 --> 00:01:01,540
parallel time.

17
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:05,628
Start with why. Simulations, data processing, model training: sooner or later one

18
00:01:05,648 --> 00:01:10,677
core isn't fast enough, so we split the work. Think of laundry. Serial: wash, dry,

19
00:01:10,697 --> 00:01:15,388
fold one load, then the next waits. Nine steps for three loads. Pipelining: while

20
00:01:15,408 --> 00:01:19,763
load one dries, load two washes. Five steps. Data parallelism: three washers, three

21
00:01:19,783 --> 00:01:24,811
dryers, three loads at once. Three steps. Most of today is data parallelism: cut a

22
00:01:24,831 --> 00:01:27,840
big problem into small ones and solve them simultaneously.

23
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:31,853
Flynn's taxonomy sorts machines by how instructions and data flow. Single or

24
00:01:31,873 --> 00:01:36,147
multiple instruction; single or multiple data. SISD is an ordinary single core. SIMD

25
00:01:36,167 --> 00:01:41,432
is one instruction over many data points at once: that is a GPU. MISD is rare,

26
00:01:41,452 --> 00:01:45,395
fault-tolerant hardware. And MIMD is many instructions over many data: a multi-node

27
00:01:45,415 --> 00:01:50,680
cluster, each task on its own data. Today's two are SIMD, the GPU, and MIMD, the

28
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:51,010
cluster.

29
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:55,695
How processors reach memory decides which tool you use. Shared memory: the cores of

30
00:01:55,715 --> 00:02:01,324
one node read one pool of RAM, any core any address. Easier to program; the tool is

31
00:02:01,344 --> 00:02:06,290
OpenMP; the limit is one machine. Distributed memory: each node has its own RAM, and

32
00:02:06,310 --> 00:02:11,588
node A can't read node B's. Programs send data over the network; the tool is MPI;

33
00:02:11,608 --> 00:02:16,886
the gain is that you scale across nodes. Most real HPC codes are hybrid: MPI between

34
00:02:16,906 --> 00:02:18,210
nodes, OpenMP inside each.

35
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:23,239
This maps straight onto Slurm. n tasks is the number of processes, the MPI ranks.

36
00:02:23,259 --> 00:02:28,218
CPUs per task is the cores each process gets for its threads. They multiply: four

37
00:02:28,238 --> 00:02:33,529
tasks per node, five CPUs per task, is twenty cores per node. Pure OpenMP is one

38
00:02:33,549 --> 00:02:38,508
task with twenty CPUs. Pure MPI is twenty tasks with one CPU each. Hybrid sits

39
00:02:38,528 --> 00:02:38,840
between.

40
00:02:38,910 --> 00:02:41,999
Now the speed limit. Not everything parallelises: reading data, writing results,

41
00:02:42,019 --> 00:02:46,521
steps that depend on the last. Amdahl's law: speedup equals one over S plus P over

42
00:02:46,541 --> 00:02:51,043
N, where S is the serial fraction, P the parallel fraction, and N the number of

43
00:02:51,063 --> 00:02:55,848
processors. If ten percent of your code is serial, the most you can ever get is ten

44
00:02:55,868 --> 00:02:59,522
times, however many processors you add. The curve climbs, then flattens. This is

45
00:02:59,542 --> 00:03:01,500
strong scaling: the same problem, more cores.

46
00:03:01,570 --> 00:03:05,796
Gustafson asked the other question: what if the problem grows with the cores? A

47
00:03:05,816 --> 00:03:09,738
researcher with a hundred cores doesn't finish a ten-minute problem in six seconds.

48
00:03:09,758 --> 00:03:14,287
They solve a problem a hundred times larger in the same ten minutes. Speedup equals

49
00:03:14,307 --> 00:03:19,745
S plus P times N. That is weak scaling, and it's how most real HPC work gets done.

50
00:03:19,765 --> 00:03:23,991
Both laws are right; they answer different questions. One tip before any code: plan

51
00:03:24,011 --> 00:03:27,630
what can be parallel. Retrofitting parallelism onto finished serial code is painful.

52
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:32,450
Now a real case. Conway's Game of Life: a grid where each cell's next state depends

53
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:36,325
on its eight neighbours. It is embarrassingly parallel, because within one time step

54
00:03:36,345 --> 00:03:41,095
every cell is independent of every other. A single core walks the grid row by row.

55
00:03:41,115 --> 00:03:43,480
Many cores can take the rows at once.

56
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:48,576
With OpenMP, one directive above the loop asks the compiler to spread it across the

57
00:03:48,596 --> 00:03:53,622
cores of a node. The pragma collapses the two loops into one parallel space; the

58
00:03:53,642 --> 00:03:55,640
compiler creates and distributes the threads.

59
00:03:55,710 --> 00:04:01,401
Submitting it: one node, one task, twenty CPUs per task, and the thread count set

60
00:04:01,421 --> 00:04:05,970
from Slurm's variable. Ten thousand by ten thousand cells, five hundred generations.

61
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:09,491
When the grid outgrows one node's memory, split it across nodes: domain

62
00:04:09,511 --> 00:04:13,830
decomposition. The grid is cut into horizontal strips, one per MPI rank. But a cell

63
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:18,458
on the edge of a strip needs neighbours from the strip next door, on another node.

64
00:04:18,478 --> 00:04:22,797
So before each step the ranks swap their boundary rows with MPI send and receive.

65
00:04:22,817 --> 00:04:25,400
Those borrowed rows are the halo, or ghost zone.

66
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:30,186
The hybrid job: four nodes, four ranks per node, five threads per rank. Four times

67
00:04:30,206 --> 00:04:35,554
four times five is eighty cores, launched with m p i run and the task count from

68
00:04:35,574 --> 00:04:35,870
Slurm.

69
00:04:35,940 --> 00:04:39,939
The real numbers, same grid, five hundred iterations. Serial: two hundred fifty

70
00:04:39,959 --> 00:04:44,627
seconds. OpenMP on two cores, two point two times faster. Four cores, four point

71
00:04:44,647 --> 00:04:49,651
three. Eight, eight and a half. Twelve, only seven and a half, slower than eight.

72
00:04:49,671 --> 00:04:54,004
Sixteen, ten times. Twenty, twelve point four. Then across nodes with MPI: forty

73
00:04:54,024 --> 00:04:57,353
cores, twenty-five times. Sixty, thirty-seven. Eighty, forty-four. Inside one node

74
00:04:57,373 --> 00:05:02,377
the scaling is close to ideal, with no network in the way. Across nodes the

75
00:05:02,397 --> 00:05:06,730
communication cost shows, and the curve bends under the ideal line, exactly as

76
00:05:06,750 --> 00:05:07,400
Amdahl predicted.

77
00:05:07,470 --> 00:05:12,488
GPUs. A CPU is built for low latency: tens of complex cores, good at sequential

78
00:05:12,508 --> 00:05:17,191
logic and branching. A GPU is built for throughput: thousands of simple cores doing

79
00:05:17,211 --> 00:05:21,222
the same operation across a large array. A few professional chefs cooking

80
00:05:21,242 --> 00:05:25,924
complicated dishes, against a thousand line cooks each doing one simple task for a

81
00:05:25,944 --> 00:05:26,260
buffet.

82
00:05:26,330 --> 00:05:31,144
Every GPU program follows four steps. Allocate memory on the device. Copy the data

83
00:05:31,164 --> 00:05:36,670
from host to device, CPU to GPU. Launch a kernel: thousands of threads run the same

84
00:05:36,690 --> 00:05:41,849
function on different data. Then copy the results back. Host is the CPU; device is

85
00:05:41,869 --> 00:05:42,540
the GPU.

86
00:05:42,610 --> 00:05:46,908
A matrix-multiply kernel. Each thread works out its own row and column from its

87
00:05:46,928 --> 00:05:51,227
block and thread indices, and computes exactly one element of the result. For a

88
00:05:51,247 --> 00:05:54,928
thousand-by-thousand matrix that is over a million threads, in parallel. You may

89
00:05:54,948 --> 00:05:59,863
never write Kooda yourself: PyTorch, CuPy and RAPIDS do it for you. But this is what

90
00:05:59,883 --> 00:06:00,480
they do.

91
00:06:00,550 --> 00:06:06,248
In PyTorch the pattern is: put the tensors on the device, and the library calls the

92
00:06:06,268 --> 00:06:10,179
Kooda kernels for you. Two four-thousand-square matrices and one matmul. Submitted

93
00:06:10,199 --> 00:06:12,680
with one GPU on the gpu partition.

94
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:17,961
More than one GPU on a node: split one big operation across them. Partition A by

95
00:06:17,981 --> 00:06:23,845
rows, give every GPU its shard and a full copy of B, launch the matmuls, they run in

96
00:06:23,865 --> 00:06:27,115
parallel because kernel launches are asynchronous, then synchronise each device.

97
00:06:27,135 --> 00:06:29,730
Submitted with two GPUs on the multigpu partition.

98
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:34,122
Across nodes, PyTorch uses Distributed Data Parallel. The vocabulary: the backend is

99
00:06:34,142 --> 00:06:38,827
NCCL, NVIDIA's communication library, MPI's cousin for GPUs. World size is the total

100
00:06:38,847 --> 00:06:44,979
number of GPUs. Rank is each GPU's ID. Local rank is its index inside its node. The

101
00:06:44,999 --> 00:06:50,045
communication test: every GPU holds a one, all-reduce sums them, and if the result

102
00:06:50,065 --> 00:06:52,940
equals the world size, every GPU is talking.

103
00:06:53,010 --> 00:06:57,118
The GPU benchmark: a four-thousand-square matmul, a hundred times. One GPU, eleven

104
00:06:57,138 --> 00:07:02,623
point seven seconds. Two GPUs on one node, five and a half seconds: two point one

105
00:07:02,643 --> 00:07:07,439
times, near linear over the fast in-node link. Four GPUs across two nodes, five

106
00:07:07,459 --> 00:07:12,944
point one seconds: only two point three times. At this size each GPU gets a tiny

107
00:07:12,964 --> 00:07:16,728
shard, and the cross-node sync costs more than the maths. The

108
00:07:16,748 --> 00:07:19,480
computation-to-communication ratio decides how far you can scale.

109
00:07:19,550 --> 00:07:24,493
You don't need C or Kooda to go parallel. In Python, mpi4py gives you MPI: a

110
00:07:24,513 --> 00:07:29,145
communicator, your rank and the size of the world, each rank working on its own

111
00:07:29,165 --> 00:07:34,418
chunk, and a reduce to rank zero. Launch it with m p i run. Also worth knowing:

112
00:07:34,438 --> 00:07:37,210
multiprocessing for one node, concurrent futures, Dask and Ray.

113
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:42,749
In R, the parallel package ships with base R: make a cluster of all cores but one,

114
00:07:42,769 --> 00:07:47,269
par l apply over your list, stop the cluster. Or for-each with do-parallel for

115
00:07:47,289 --> 00:07:50,820
embarrassingly parallel loops: register four cores and swap do for do-par.

116
00:07:50,890 --> 00:07:56,728
In MATLAB, parpool opens a pool of workers and parfor replaces a for loop. MATLAB

117
00:07:56,748 --> 00:07:57,900
distributes the iterations.

118
00:07:57,970 --> 00:08:03,062
Choosing the tool. OpenMP: shared memory, loop-level parallelism on one node. MPI:

119
00:08:03,082 --> 00:08:06,471
distributed, multi-node simulations. mpi4py for multi-node Python. multiprocessing

120
00:08:06,491 --> 00:08:12,861
for single-node Python tasks. PyTorch DDP for training. Dask or Ray for big data and

121
00:08:12,881 --> 00:08:17,974
pipelines, both memory models. R's parallel and foreach, and MATLAB's parfor, for

122
00:08:17,994 --> 00:08:20,530
embarrassingly parallel loops on one node.

123
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:24,723
Four things to keep. Profile first: find the bottleneck before you parallelise.

124
00:08:24,743 --> 00:08:29,212
Choose the level: shared memory on one node, MPI across nodes, GPUs for

125
00:08:29,232 --> 00:08:33,355
data-parallel maths. Communication costs are real: more nodes are not always faster.

126
00:08:33,375 --> 00:08:38,879
And plan for parallelism from the start. Questions: r c help at northeastern dot e d

127
00:08:38,899 --> 00:08:40,260
u, or office hours.

128
00:08:40,330 --> 00:08:46,375
And the three Slurm recipes to keep. OpenMP: one node, one task, twenty CPUs. MPI

129
00:08:46,395 --> 00:08:52,036
plus OpenMP: four nodes, four ranks each, five threads each. GPU: one node, two

130
00:08:52,056 --> 00:08:52,440
GPUs.

131
00:08:52,510 --> 00:08:56,780
Let's recap with Claude. Pipelining and data parallelism, and today was data

132
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:01,428
parallelism. SIMD is the GPU, MIMD is the cluster; shared memory means OpenMP,

133
00:09:01,448 --> 00:09:06,076
distributed means MPI. Amdahl: ten percent serial caps you at ten times. Gustafson:

134
00:09:06,096 --> 00:09:11,796
grow the problem with the cores. The Game of Life ran twelve times faster on twenty

135
00:09:11,816 --> 00:09:16,087
cores and forty-four times on eighty across nodes, with communication bending the

136
00:09:16,107 --> 00:09:21,092
curve. A GPU program allocates, copies in, launches the kernel and copies back; two

137
00:09:21,112 --> 00:09:26,097
GPUs gave two point one times, four across nodes only two point three. Profile

138
00:09:26,117 --> 00:09:30,030
first, choose the level, count the communication, and plan for parallelism.

139
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:35,221
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here is a serial program I want to run faster on

140
00:09:35,241 --> 00:09:39,454
a cluster: [describe it and paste the main loop]. First, mark which parts are

141
00:09:39,474 --> 00:09:43,386
inherently serial and estimate my Amdahl ceiling. Then tell me which level fits:

142
00:09:43,406 --> 00:09:48,224
threads on one node, MPI across nodes, or a GPU, and why. Finally, write the Slurm

143
00:09:48,244 --> 00:09:51,853
header for that choice with --ntasks and --cpus-per-task explained. Then check two

144
00:09:51,873 --> 00:09:56,389
things yourself. Did it name the serial part before it promised a speedup? And do

145
00:09:56,409 --> 00:10:00,320
its n tasks times CPUs per task equal the cores you'd actually get?

146
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:04,970
Intro to Parallel Computing. At Nik Bear Brown.
